Every evening at 6, Raju uncle opened his tiny tea stall near the Huzurabad bus stand.
Same spot, same steel glasses, same 10-rupee chai for 12 years.One rainy evening, a girl ran in, soaked and crying. She had missed her last bus home to Karimnagar and lost her wallet. Her phone was dead too.Raju uncle didn’t ask questions. He just poured her a cup of chai, put a biscuit on the side, and said, "Sit. Rain will stop."She sat quietly. Between sips she told him she had a job interview tomorrow — her first one. If she missed it, she’d have to wait 3 more months.When the rain slowed, Raju uncle said, "My brother drives a goods van to Karimnagar at 5am. You can go with him. No charge."The girl was shocked. "Why are you helping me?"He smiled and wiped a glass. "Because 12 years ago, someone gave me chai when I had nothing. I’m just returning it."Next month, the same girl came back. In a saree, with a company ID card.
She placed a box of sweets on his counter. "I got the job, uncle. First salary."Raju uncle poured two cups of chai. "Then this one’s on me."Some debts aren’t paid with money. They’re paid forward — one cup at a time
